WEBVTT

00:00:11.949 --> 00:00:13.442
A replaced card still works.

00:00:15.489 --> 00:00:17.366
A subscription charges the card you saved,

00:00:17.622 --> 00:00:18.604
and you are not there.

00:00:19.116 --> 00:00:20.268
But banks replace cards:

00:00:20.694 --> 00:00:22.230
they expire, they are lost,

00:00:22.785 --> 00:00:23.485
the account closes.

00:00:24.321 --> 00:00:25.473
When the saved card is dead,

00:00:25.857 --> 00:00:27.350
trying it again almost never works:

00:00:27.905 --> 00:00:29.057
about one time in seventy.

00:00:32.146 --> 00:00:34.109
Two tools can keep the subscription billing.

00:00:34.578 --> 00:00:35.278
The card updater:

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.351
the card network tells us the new number,

00:00:37.863 --> 00:00:38.563
or the new date.

00:00:38.845 --> 00:00:39.570
And the network token:

00:00:39.997 --> 00:00:41.874
a stand-in number that the network itself

00:00:42.258 --> 00:00:44.221
keeps current when the bank reissues the card.

00:00:47.441 --> 00:00:49.574
The updater works today on one of our processors.

00:00:50.427 --> 00:00:53.030
In one month, Worldpay sent us more than

00:00:53.073 --> 00:00:54.438
twelve hundred answers:

00:00:54.779 --> 00:00:56.443
a new number, a new date,

00:00:56.827 --> 00:00:57.595
or account closed.

00:00:58.101 --> 00:00:59.936
On our other paths no answer can come

00:00:59.978 --> 00:01:02.112
back, because the updater only watches

00:01:02.154 --> 00:01:03.520
cards stored at the processor,

00:01:03.946 --> 00:01:05.696
and ours are stored in our own vault.

00:01:06.293 --> 00:01:09.152
About three in four rebills run on those paths.

00:01:12.539 --> 00:01:14.502
The token already works for us in one place:

00:01:14.886 --> 00:01:15.586
Apple Pay.

00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:17.531
Its rebills ride on a network token:

00:01:17.958 --> 00:01:19.793
more than four thousand in three weeks,

00:01:20.219 --> 00:01:21.798
and not one needed an updater.

00:01:25.113 --> 00:01:26.563
What comes next is planned,

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:27.519
not built:

00:01:27.758 --> 00:01:30.489
our own network tokens, stored beside each

00:01:30.617 --> 00:01:32.451
card in our own vault, so that they

00:01:32.494 --> 00:01:33.603
work at every processor.

00:01:34.457 --> 00:01:36.078
The card number stays as the fallback.

00:01:39.347 --> 00:01:41.950
Until then, the surest fix is a different card:

00:01:42.291 --> 00:01:44.552
after a dead card, a new one pays

00:01:44.638 --> 00:01:45.619
four times in five.

00:01:47.933 --> 00:01:48.701
A replaced card:

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:51.005
the updater today on one processor,

00:01:51.474 --> 00:01:52.840
the token today on Apple Pay,

00:01:53.224 --> 00:01:54.818
and our own tokens next.

